121:### 1b. Opus-habit bank (default lever picks; 24 Oct second read of the 6 delivered tasks)
122:Delivered scores are bimodal per lever: about 96 to 99 when the agent reads its main rule strictly and 41 to 72 when it does not. The misses that repeat across tasks are habits, not puzzles. Pick each lever's wrong reading from this list:
131:- [v3: g1 GS:5-10, g3/C_0b27b9dc.md:65] H9 shipped-only self-test: checks aggregates and invariants on shipped data, never builds an input that fires a stated branch (S2, S8, and the probe band).
135:- H1: bare type property ("`units`: a JSON integer") beside a global trim or normalising rule. Never list the malformed values and never write a carve-out sentence naming the exception. precheck.sh fails LV-ENUM when a contract lists the malformed values of a typed field.
139:- Stateful keys (H6, group counters keyed wider than the habit key) held as hidden-only cases are the strongest stated lever measured so far.
144:## 2. Writing rules. Every rule of engagement stated (IN-15); every graded rule has one reading (IN-16), from a sentence or from determinate evidence (0b).
145:- W1. Never explain the trap. No "not only X", no "note that", no trap lists, no rule counts, no sentence explaining why a carve-out exists or restating the consequence of a definition. QC fails the same act as IN-17 (01:47) in instruction.md. Our W1 stays stricter than IN-17: counts and trap lists are banned in every agent-visible file. [v3: kept; a data-side trap list passed QC in 4e2f6925 and gave no gain, g4/C_4e2f6925.md:10, :17; CONFLICTS C6]
149:- W5. Stated levers: shipped data includes the separating case for L5 only; for the others the separating cases are in hidden reseeded inputs only, and the shipped data never contradicts them. [v3: S2, g1 GS:87-93] For Shape D this is the design goal: write `WORK/BRANCH_FIRE.md`, one row per stated branch: `| rule | branch | fires on shipped (count) | fires on hidden (seeds) |`; the hard branch has shipped count 0 and fires on every hidden corpus that grades it. L11 inverts this: the shipped input MUST hold one separating witness.
150:- W6. [v3: CONFLICTS C7, g2/C_37a0c3d6.md:19, :44] A worked example may show one rule on invented toy values that appear in no shipped or hidden file, at most one per rule. It never uses a shipped record or a graded figure and never says which case is the trap. No other worked example in which the right and wrong readings differ, except the single unlabelled L11 witness.
155:- W11. No answer for any graded document is visible to the agent (Ayush decision 5): no answer key, golden or sample output, solved list, expected figure, claimed total or "the correct result is" sentence for any graded document, anywhere in `environment/`. Allowed: honest controls per W7, toy worked examples per W6, the single unlabelled L11 witness. An output-shape example (FC:492) carries placeholder or out-of-scope values.
156:- [v3: g1 GS:46-52, 6d12f970] W12. When the generator re-renders rule documents per hidden corpus (dates, ids, thresholds), the rendered sentences stay word-for-word except the values, and the rule register lists which values change.
158:## 3. Grading rules
160:- G1. Whole-document exact grading per hidden corpus. Tests compare each output document whole, exactly, on each hidden corpus, provided instruction.md states that only an exact match counts (G5); quote FC:491 in QC_DEFENCE under CL-4. Report the one-fact-wrong score anyway. Every amplified slip must trace to a stated single-reading rule or an L11 rule (a GENUINE loss), never to an ambiguity, an unstated rule or an oracle defect (G9). A COUPLING quantity still multiplies a slip legitimately. [v3: CONFLICTS C3; g2/C_37a0c3d6.md:48 QC passed a stated 9-corpus gate; g1 GS:46-52 b42c61b9 WARN] At most one stated cross-corpus gate per task ("a family counts only when every one of its corpora is exact"), with its sentence in instruction.md; the related QC checks (CL-4, TS-19, AL-28) must pass; and every key, field and value form any test reads must be named somewhere in the shipped contract (minimal explanation is fine). Never grade a key the agent cannot read. [QL 26 Oct, Q3: "search for related checks in qc and if they pass it passes. the concept is to not test on hidden keys, if you want to test then at least mention the keys in contract somewhere, no or minimal explanation is fine."]
161:- G1a. [v3: g2/C_3def42a3.md:62-64] Per-row ownership (the safe alternative to a gate). Each output row or byte is owned by exactly one test (`OWNED` map); shared columns are masked in every test except their owner. One slip then costs its rows in one test, and a test that compared nothing never passes (`_tier_ok`, g2/C_3def42a3.md:67).
163:- G3. [v3: CONFLICTS C2, g1 GS:23, g2 GS:47-58] Hidden share follows the shape. Shape D: at least 70% of positive weight on generated hidden corpora. Shape B: no floor; shipped whole-family exact may carry the weight. Every shape: at least one hidden rerun per output family. Shares are measured by following the call graph (section 5), never by test names.
164:- G4. Negatives grade only cheating acts (hardcoding, editing outside scope, reading tests/, network or third-party imports). Group by act, <= 5 per group across both layers (AL-28). No negative grades a lever; no wrong-reading detector ("output equals the flip"). [v3: kept, CONFLICTS C4; 16 detectors fired 0 times g4 GS:66-77, one fired falsely g2 GS:47-58] Every negative quotes, in QC_DEFENCE, the sentence that states its rule (03:50 carve-out, 05:21). [v3: g4 GS:102-105] No negative depends on the program's exit code alone (an rc-sensitive negative fired on an honest run).
166:- G6. At least 2 mutation tests. Stock recipes: shuffle rows + scale one rate curve by a non-round factor; relocate or rename the corpus directory; rotate row order; delete one declared source (expect a non-zero exit naming it); run as non-root from a foreign cwd; run twice and compare. [v3: CONFLICTS C15, 0b27b9dc test_output.py:4386-4457, g3 GS:21] A mutation or variant test counts only on corpora where the plain hidden run of the same family is exact, so one slip is not charged twice.
168:- G8. Hidden tier: an embedded seeded generator in tests/ builds the hidden inputs; the agent program re-runs on them as a foreign uid. Hidden seeds change every named release literal. For L11 the generator plants a fresh witness per corpus. No slug or author string inside SHA-pinned test text. [v3: g2 GS:28] Hidden-only fixtures (ties, rare branches) use their own RNG stream so adding one never shifts other corpora.
171:- G10. [v3: CONFLICTS C3] Cost table (`WORK/COST_TABLE.md`). For each rule (Shape B) or lever (Shape D), inject its wrong reading alone into a patched solve.py, run the real tests, record test points lost. Gate Shape B: at least 15 rules each lose >= 3%, and the sum of the 15 cheapest-to-miss rows is >= 40%. Gate Shape D: every lever alone loses >= 25%. Both: every "all but one right" row has predicted final < 70 using the design judge estimate (section 6), not a fixed 90. These are design numbers, not hardness evidence. Differential fuzz of an EARLIER agent program against a CHANGED bundle is not evidence of anything (Ayush decision 11).
172:- G11. Rule coverage (AL-27). `WORK/RULE_COVERAGE.md` has one row per AL-2 unit and per ordering, precedence or tie-break the shipped input establishes (05:38): `| rule | file:line | mutant | tests failed |`. Each mutant drops that rule alone from the oracle; every row fails at least 1 test. Tie-break and ordering rules need a planted tie in the hidden tier.
173:- G12. The embedded hidden generator asserts every value it draws is inside the bounds the contract states or the input determines (TS-19), on every seed the tests use, and plants every branch of each multi-branch rule on some seed. The reference and generator survive 300 seeds with no crash or endless loop. [v3: b42c61b9 thread-unsafe cache, oracle 9/67 before fix, g1 GS:72-77] No shared mutable cache across tests.
175:- Counts: no cap on tests or criteria; the QC WARN above 35 (TS-23 03:62) is recorded, not fixed. TS-20: core weights never 1.
177:## 4. Judge rules
178:Judge goal: the judge half lands near 25-45% for an Opus-like agent (g3 GS:7-11: rubric 25-33 with tests 90-96 gave finals 57-64).
180:- J2. [v3: CONFLICTS C1; g3 GS:25, g3/C_0b27b9dc.md:38, :66] Probe band. 8 to 16 probe criteria, each w3, importance "important", type "process", evaluation_target "trajectory", together 55-70% of positive judge weight. Each names ONE act Opus skips and the requirement it serves (04:92 "name the requirement the conduct SERVES"). Pick from the probe templates P-a to P-h below. The oracle shows every probe act (JD-7 04:76): solve.sh's self-check stage performs each probe act and prints `check <id> ok` plus the one witness line (the input id and the decision). [QL 26 Oct, Q1: "you may judge in traj but it should be reflected in oracle as well, so phrase your rubric so it passes oracle." A trajectory criterion is allowed only when the oracle shows it.] The agent never sees the oracle trajectory, so this costs no hardness. Never echo probe wording in instruction.md.
182:  - P-a edge-input run: "The agent runs <program> on an input it built in which <stated branch> applies, and shows the resulting <output row> for that input." (serves: the rule <id> output on fresh inputs)
183:  - P-b one-instance witness: "The agent shows at least one <item id> where <stated condition> holds together with the decision its program made for it." (serves: <family> exactness)
184:  - P-c recompute outside: "The agent recomputes <one named figure kind> for at least one <item> by a method separate from <program> and compares the two values." (one per artifact type; g3 GS:26)
185:  - P-d altered-copy rerun: "The agent runs <program> on a copy of the inputs in which it changed <a value the rule reads>, and shows that <dependent output> changed." (not an exact copy: 56fe3bdd R7 failed on an exact copy, g3/C_56fe3bdd.md:55)
186:  - P-e relocated rerun: "The agent runs <program> from a directory other than the shipped one, on inputs at a path other than the shipped one." (3 pts in 2 of 2 runs, g2 GS:33-36)
187:  - P-f broken tool first (repair tasks): "Before its first edit to <tool>, the agent runs <tool> and shows its failing behaviour." (8d3e20fb, g4 GS:49-55)
188:  - P-g uniqueness witness (S3): "The agent shows one input where more than one <candidate> was checked and states how many fit." 
189:  - P-h empty or malformed input: "The agent runs <program> on an input with <a declared file missing> and shows what the program does." (e13fd332 R3, g3 GS:29)
191:  - no negative on a file the agent hand-created in out/ when the program itself is the out/ deliverable (0b R6 fired on `cp`);
192:  - no confinement clause that widens to "the agent's session" (e13 R1);
198:  - no criterion that lists a menu of acts ("runs on a constructed corpus of kind X, Y or Z") unless each kind is its own w1 criterion (6d12f970 agent did 3 of 7, g1 GS:30-33).
200:  - J3a: "The response claims a check, test or re-run passed with no run after the agent's last edit to <program> in the trajectory that shows it passing" (w -3). [v3.4: Ayush decision 7] Present in every judge; it also covers a claim that outputs are byte-identical or unchanged with no such run (8 of 21 delivered runs claimed all checks pass, research cb612aa). Disjoint from every other negative (AL-28).
201:  - J3b: [v3: 4e2f6925 12.82 rubric pts, g4 GS:49-55] "The response states a figure for <output family> that differs from the value in the delivered file it names" (w -5; final_answer target; oracle-safe because the oracle reply quotes its files).
203:- J4. [v3: CONFLICTS C14; 8764bfdd 3/18 vs 80877080 passed, g4 GS:49-55; JD-18 04:99] Exactly one code-quality criterion when the deliverable is code, behavioural: "The agent leaves <path> in which every exception handler acts on the error it catches (raises, logs and exits non-zero, or records it in an output), and a missing or malformed declared input stops the run with a message naming the file." The oracle must meet it line by line (QC_RULINGS cohort v3:38 "fix the oracle, not the criterion").
212:- Kit standing of each part: probes w3 = odds-raising, pass under JD-15 Step 2 (04:92 "an odds-raising approach weighted 1 or 3 passes"); oracle shows them (JD-7 kept); trajectory observable differs from every test (JD-8 04:77, 04:81 "artifact vs trajectory"); J10 quotes a sentence (JD-16 04:93-97); J4 lists only JD-18 items (04:99). The probe wording ("shows one instance", "runs on an input where <stated branch> applies") passes as odds-raising per QL 26 Oct, Q2: "yes, pass." (QC_RULINGS.md section "QL answers 26 Oct 2026").
214:## 5. STACKING_CHECK gate (every build, harden and fix round, before P11)
216:- (a) One row per test: id, weight, observable, decisive logic, rules or levers whose misread fails it, corpora it runs (shipped / hidden seed list), gate helpers it passes through. No two rows share both observable and logic (TS-26). One property per test (TS-13).
219:- (d) Cost table (G10) with the Shape B or Shape D gate result.
220:- (e) Rule coverage (G11) and RULE_TRACE (0b): row counts, any row with 0 tests failed, any generator branch with no sentence.
222:- (g) [v3] BRANCH_FIRE (W5) for Shape D: every hard branch fires 0 times on shipped data.
223:- (h) [v3] JUDGE_MAP: probe count 8-16, probe share 55-70% of positive judge weight, every criterion with an oracle-shows entry.
227:## 6. Probe gate and evidence rules (Ayush decisions 7, 8, 11; kill rule 26 Oct)
228:- [v3: P2 design-stage blind read, our 3-minute transliterations, registry/tasks.json:707-734] Design-stage blind read: before the full build, one P13 session reads only the draft instruction.md and the rule register with a small shipped sample and writes its program. If that program matches the oracle on the shipped sample AND on 3 hidden seeds, the design is redone before the full build.
236:- Harden decisions rest on 1 real harness run plus that round's blind reads. [v3: CONFLICTS C16, Ayush D2 26 Oct] The final gate: tests < 100 AND final < 70. [v3.6: Ayush 27 Oct 22:20 "new gate is test < 85 , rubrics < 85 and overall < 50"] REPLACED: the final gate is now tests < 85 AND rubric < 85 AND final < 50 on that 1 valid run (final = (tests + rubric)/2, so final < 50 needs rubric < 100 - tests: tests 80 needs rubric < 20). [v3.7: Ayush 28 Oct "Target <40 now"] REPLACED again: the final gate is tests < 85 AND rubric < 85 AND final reward < 40 on 1 valid run of the frozen QC-passed bytes before push (v3.7, Ayush 28 Oct "Target <40 now"; was 50 under v3.6, Ayush 27 Oct 22:20; was tests < 100 AND final < 70 before). Final = (tests + rubric)/2, so final < 40 needs rubric < 80 - tests; design for tests <= 60 and rubric <= 20. Tasks that met the old gate and miss the new one are hardened from their trajectory (KNOWLEDGE section T) with the proven levers (1C) and the QC-safe edits; judge target is a probe band with predicted mid 15-20. [v3.4: Ayush 27 Oct 17:17 "push needs only 1 run that is QC passed and under our gate score"] Push needs 1 valid harness run on the frozen QC-passed bytes (tree sha recorded at freeze) that meets the gate; any change after that run voids it. [v3.1: QL Sugam 26 Oct 18:55 answered "no" to "is a task deliverable with tests 100 and final < 70 via the rubric"; Ayush D2 reverted] A judge-only rebuild never rescues a tests-100 task; at least one positive test must be lost.
238:- [v3.2: Ayush 26 Oct 19:00 "just do not directly and more park task as we have to create and push it"] NO AUTO-PARK. Every outcome that the old kill rule parked (tests 100, or final above 70) now triggers a test-hardening round (sources T1 quiet branches = S6, T2 breadth = Shape B, T3 whole-doc + per-row ownership = G1/G1a, T4 rare hidden fixtures; target tests 70-90), issued with Ayush's override past the 3-round limit. A task is parked only on Ayush's word. The old rule below is kept for the gate arithmetic only.
240:- [v3.3: Ayush 27 Oct, own iteration evidence] Measure and order. The fresh blind read (3 P13 sessions, never the old agent program or a worker reflex program) is the only measure of a round; a round counts only when fresh blind programs lose at least 10% of positive test weight. The design blind also tries the strongest generic solver for the core (bitset subset-sum, meet-in-the-middle, small-width DP, sweep); if it is exact within the time limit, redesign before the full build. One QC runs before the first probe, and every finding, minors included, is fixed in one pass.
241:- [v3.4: Ayush decisions 1, 5, 6, 8; 27 Oct] Order and run use. Design -> build (with precheck self-checks) -> 3 fresh blind reads -> one full QC with fix-all -> QC recheck rounds -> freeze -> 1 harness run. No hardening after a QC pass. If the run misses the gate, never re-run the same bytes (same-bytes spread is about ±17.5 tests and ±20 final, sentinel attempts 5/6); the master first writes the trajectory read-out (KNOWLEDGE.md section T: TRIP table, why it scored, QC-safe edits), then a fix prompt, then QC recheck, then 1 new run. `harbor trials regrade` of a saved run is a diagnostic Ayush may run; it is never push evidence and never packaged. [v3.7 regrade exception, Ayush 28 Oct Q1] A regrade counts as push evidence only when the edit touches judge files only (no tests/, no instruction, no environment), the run was valid, Ayush wrote OK for this regrade (verbatim in decisions.jsonl), and the regraded run is repackaged with `master_chat_tasks/tools/repackage_one.py` (A4). Otherwise diagnostic only, never packaged.
243:- Not evidence: differential fuzz of an old program on a changed bundle; a per-lever claim from one run; oracle-injection numbers presented as agent behaviour; a blind read's self-marked judge PASS without the step it cites.
244:- A score from a run with a grader error (permission, crash, timeout, missing file) is never a baseline. Only `valid: true` lines in `registry/scores.jsonl` count.
