58:- A time or memory limit in the instruction, tests or judge is at least 3x the oracle's measured time on the hidden inputs, measured in this session, or there is no limit.
60:## Score conventions
62:- Gate for real model runs (QL 26 Oct 18:55, D2 reverted; Ayush 27 Oct 17:17): tests < 85 AND rubric < 85 AND final reward < 40 on 1 valid run of the frozen QC-passed bytes before push (v3.7, Ayush 28 Oct "Target <40 now"; was 50 under v3.6, Ayush 27 Oct 22:20; was tests < 100 AND final < 70 before). Final = (tests + rubric)/2, so final < 40 needs rubric < 80 - tests; design for tests <= 60 and rubric <= 20.
63:- Counts (Ayush 24 Oct 12:50, repeated: "there is not cap on number of test and rubrics"): no upper cap on tests or judge criteria. Kit floors stay: at least 10 tests and 8 criteria (FAIL below). Above 35 tests the kit prints WARN A3/TS-23: record it with the count, never merge or drop a check to get under 35.
64:- QC target (Ayush 24 Oct 12:50): "pass the QC with genuine and minimum minors or warn". Zero fails; every minor or warn left must be genuine, and as few as possible. Fix a REAL minor or warn when the fix keeps every lever; otherwise KEEP it with verifiable evidence in PL_NOTE.md. Delivered peer tasks shipped with accepted warns (Final_Delivery reads 24 Oct). This supersedes the dec 101 zero-warn rule and the 35/35 caps.
65:- Hardness is proved by a real probe run on the harness, which the master queues, scored per check by `tools/loss.py`, after 3 blind reads (LEVER_RULES v3 section 6: a rule counts only when at least 2 of 3 blind programs get it wrong; each blind read also reports a judge estimate with lower and upper bounds; harden or redesign at design, never park, when the lower-bound final is above 70 on 2 of 3). A design-stage blind read runs before the full build (P2 Phase 0b). Reflex programs, per-lever solvers and differential fuzz of an old program on a changed bundle are not hardness evidence. Harden decisions: 1 run plus blind reads; final gate: 1 run on frozen QC-passed bytes. No auto-park (Ayush 26 Oct 19:00): tests 100 or final above 70 trigger a test-hardening round (LEVER_RULES v3.2), never a park; only Ayush parks a task. Old kill rule, kept for arithmetic: park when the probe's final reward (tests and judge combined) is above 70; tests at 100 on 2 valid runs park the task (QL 26 Oct); otherwise tests alone never park a task; single runs swing about 20 test points (G13), so a final between 65 and 75 gets a second probe and parks when the mean is above 70; no round after a round marked LAST.
66:- Order (Ayush decision 1, 27 Oct): design -> build (precheck self-checks) -> 3 fresh blind reads -> one full QC with fix-all -> QC recheck rounds -> freeze -> 1 harness run. A missed gate is never re-run on the same bytes (decision 6): the same bytes spread about ±17.5 tests and ±20 final (sentinel attempts 5 and 6), so a re-run measures luck. `harbor trials regrade` is a diagnostic only, never push evidence. [v3.7 regrade exception, Ayush 28 Oct Q1] A regrade counts as push evidence only when the edit touches judge files only (no tests/, no instruction, no environment), the run was valid, Ayush wrote OK for this regrade (verbatim in decisions.jsonl), and the regraded run is repackaged with `master_chat_tasks/tools/repackage_one.py` (A4). Otherwise diagnostic only, never packaged.
67:- Packaging integrity: a pushed bundle is packaged from exactly the frozen bytes the gate run used; Output_RFP `tests/` equals the frozen `tests/`, and the scores come from that run (scripts/package_rfp_bundles.py copies scores without recomputing, line 73; never package a regrade, except under the v3.7 regrade exception above).
68:- Baselines: a score from a run with a grader error (permission, crash, timeout, missing file) is never a baseline. Only `valid: true` lines of `master_chat_tasks/registry/scores.jsonl` count.
