## 6. Probe gate and evidence rules (Ayush decisions 7, 8, 11; kill rule 26 Oct)
- [v3: P2 design-stage blind read, our 3-minute transliterations, registry/tasks.json:707-734] Design-stage blind read: before the full build, one P13 session reads only the draft instruction.md and the rule register with a small shipped sample and writes its program. If that program matches the oracle on the shipped sample AND on 3 hidden seeds, the design is redone before the full build.
- Blind read before every probe: 3 fresh P13 blind-solve sessions (each sees only a copy of instruction.md and environment/). Score each program with the real tests and `tools/loss.py`. A rule or lever counts only when at least 2 of the 3 programs get it wrong.
- [v3: new] Blind judge estimate. After its program and outputs are final, each blind session receives judge.toml and marks every criterion PASS, FAIL or UNSURE, citing the step or file that shows it (P13). The master computes, per session:
  - judge lower bound = (points of PASS criteria, probes counted PASS only when the blind CHECKS log shows the exact act) minus (fired negatives) over positive points;
  - judge upper bound = (PASS + UNSURE + every probe not contradicted by the log) over positive points; negatives counted only when shown;
  - judge mid = outcome criteria as marked, probes at the peer pass rate 25% (g3/C_0b27b9dc.md:65 3 of 16; g3/C_7e0d8feb.md:41 4 of 18);
  - final bounds = (blind tests% + judge bound) / 2.
  Before a probe: when the lower-bound final is above 70 on 2 of 3 blind reads, harden or redesign before the probe (never park; v3.2 below); queue the probe otherwise.
- Harden decisions rest on 1 real harness run plus that round's blind reads. [v3: CONFLICTS C16, Ayush D2 26 Oct] The final gate: tests < 100 AND final < 70. [v3.6: Ayush 27 Oct 22:20 "new gate is test < 85 , rubrics < 85 and overall < 50"] REPLACED: the final gate is now tests < 85 AND rubric < 85 AND final < 50 on that 1 valid run (final = (tests + rubric)/2, so final < 50 needs rubric < 100 - tests: tests 80 needs rubric < 20). [v3.7: Ayush 28 Oct "Target <40 now"] REPLACED again: the final gate is tests < 85 AND rubric < 85 AND final reward < 40 on 1 valid run of the frozen QC-passed bytes before push (v3.7, Ayush 28 Oct "Target <40 now"; was 50 under v3.6, Ayush 27 Oct 22:20; was tests < 100 AND final < 70 before). Final = (tests + rubric)/2, so final < 40 needs rubric < 80 - tests; design for tests <= 60 and rubric <= 20. Tasks that met the old gate and miss the new one are hardened from their trajectory (KNOWLEDGE section T) with the proven levers (1C) and the QC-safe edits; judge target is a probe band with predicted mid 15-20. [v3.4: Ayush 27 Oct 17:17 "push needs only 1 run that is QC passed and under our gate score"] Push needs 1 valid harness run on the frozen QC-passed bytes (tree sha recorded at freeze) that meets the gate; any change after that run voids it. [v3.1: QL Sugam 26 Oct 18:55 answered "no" to "is a task deliverable with tests 100 and final < 70 via the rubric"; Ayush D2 reverted] A judge-only rebuild never rescues a tests-100 task; at least one positive test must be lost.
- Before diagnosing any run, diff the run's staged copy and ctrf test ids against the repo bundle (Phase A). A score from a stale or different tree is not a baseline (c55a1967 recorded 76.62 vs run ctrf 70.13, g3 GS:92-97).
- [v3.2: Ayush 26 Oct 19:00 "just do not directly and more park task as we have to create and push it"] NO AUTO-PARK. Every outcome that the old kill rule parked (tests 100, or final above 70) now triggers a test-hardening round (sources T1 quiet branches = S6, T2 breadth = Shape B, T3 whole-doc + per-row ownership = G1/G1a, T4 rare hidden fixtures; target tests 70-90), issued with Ayush's override past the 3-round limit. A task is parked only on Ayush's word. The old rule below is kept for the gate arithmetic only.
- (Superseded by v3.2 for parking) Kill rule (Ayush 26 Oct): park when the probe's final reward (tests and judge combined, as the harness reports it) is above 70. Tests at 100 on 2 valid runs park the task whatever the final (QL 26 Oct 18:55); otherwise tests alone never park a task: delivered peers pass with tests 85-96 and a low judge (0b27b9dc 96.5/26, 7e0d8feb 94/33, e13fd332 90/25). [v3: CONFLICTS C9, Ayush D3 26 Oct; g2/C_daaca581_DIFF.md] Variance band: above 75, park; 65 to 75, run a second probe and park when the mean final is above 70; below 65, keep. A round marked LAST is the last.
- [v3.3: Ayush 27 Oct, own iteration evidence] Measure and order. The fresh blind read (3 P13 sessions, never the old agent program or a worker reflex program) is the only measure of a round; a round counts only when fresh blind programs lose at least 10% of positive test weight. The design blind also tries the strongest generic solver for the core (bitset subset-sum, meet-in-the-middle, small-width DP, sweep); if it is exact within the time limit, redesign before the full build. One QC runs before the first probe, and every finding, minors included, is fixed in one pass.
- [v3.4: Ayush decisions 1, 5, 6, 8; 27 Oct] Order and run use. Design -> build (with precheck self-checks) -> 3 fresh blind reads -> one full QC with fix-all -> QC recheck rounds -> freeze -> 1 harness run. No hardening after a QC pass. If the run misses the gate, never re-run the same bytes (same-bytes spread is about ±17.5 tests and ±20 final, sentinel attempts 5/6); the master first writes the trajectory read-out (KNOWLEDGE.md section T: TRIP table, why it scored, QC-safe edits), then a fix prompt, then QC recheck, then 1 new run. `harbor trials regrade` of a saved run is a diagnostic Ayush may run; it is never push evidence and never packaged. [v3.7 regrade exception, Ayush 28 Oct Q1] A regrade counts as push evidence only when the edit touches judge files only (no tests/, no instruction, no environment), the run was valid, Ayush wrote OK for this regrade (verbatim in decisions.jsonl), and the regraded run is repackaged with `master_chat_tasks/tools/repackage_one.py` (A4). Otherwise diagnostic only, never packaged.
- "QC deferred" or "QC will decide" is never a pass for IN-16 (Ayush decision 8).
- Not evidence: differential fuzz of an old program on a changed bundle; a per-lever claim from one run; oracle-injection numbers presented as agent behaviour; a blind read's self-marked judge PASS without the step it cites.
- A score from a run with a grader error (permission, crash, timeout, missing file) is never a baseline. Only `valid: true` lines in `registry/scores.jsonl` count.


